\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces W25Q64FV pinout, SOIC 8 package (top view). Pin 1 is the \texttt  {/CS} chip select. The DollaTek breakout exposes pins 1, 2, 5, 6 on its header, plus VCC and GND, and ties pins 3 and 7 internally to VCC.}}{15}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:w25q-pinout}{{9}{15}{W25Q64FV pinout, SOIC 8 package (top view). Pin 1 is the \texttt {/CS} chip select. The DollaTek breakout exposes pins 1, 2, 5, 6 on its header, plus VCC and GND, and ties pins 3 and 7 internally to VCC}{figure.caption.11}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {5.1.1}Inside the NOR cell}{16}{subsubsection.5.1.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Cross section of a NOR floating gate cell. The trapped charge on the floating gate shifts the threshold of the transistor. Programming pushes electrons from the channel up into the floating gate by Fowler Nordheim tunneling at high V\_CG. Erasing pulls them back down with the opposite polarity. The current driven through these gate dielectrics is where the chip's ``program current'' and ``erase current'' peaks come from.}}{16}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:nor-cell}{{10}{16}{Cross section of a NOR floating gate cell. The trapped charge on the floating gate shifts the threshold of the transistor. Programming pushes electrons from the channel up into the floating gate by Fowler Nordheim tunneling at high V\_CG. Erasing pulls them back down with the opposite polarity. The current driven through these gate dielectrics is where the chip's ``program current'' and ``erase current'' peaks come from}{figure.caption.12}{}}
